From: k30pro-linux-dualboot
Subject: [PATCH 0019] terminal: on-screen-keyboard input returns to the live view

key_handler() snaps the scrollback view back to the live end whenever a key is
pressed, but the on-screen keyboard delivers everything through
zwp_text_input_v1 (preedit/commit/keysym) and none of those touched the view:
typing while scrolled left the shell output and the prompt below the visible
viewport, so it looked as if the output had been swallowed.  Mirror the
key-handler reset in all three handlers (the pinyin keyboard stays in preedit
until a candidate is chosen, so preedit matters most in practice).

--- clients/terminal.c.orig
+++ clients/terminal.c
@@ -2670,6 +2670,22 @@
 {
 	struct terminal *terminal = data;
 
+	/* lmi: on-screen-keyboard input must return the view to the live end exactly
+	 * like a physical key press does (see key_handler) - otherwise typing while
+	 * the scrollback is scrolled leaves the new output below the view and the
+	 * prompt looks swallowed.  The pinyin keyboard only sends preedit events
+	 * until a candidate is chosen, so cover those too. */
+	if (terminal->scrolling) {
+		int d = terminal->saved_start - terminal->start;
+
+		terminal->row -= d;
+		terminal->selection_start_row -= d;
+		terminal->selection_end_row -= d;
+		terminal->start = terminal->saved_start;
+		terminal->scrolling = 0;
+		widget_schedule_redraw(terminal->widget);
+	}
+
 	terminal_write(terminal, text, strlen(text));
 }
 
@@ -2679,6 +2695,22 @@
 		  uint32_t state, uint32_t modifiers)
 {
 	struct terminal *terminal = data;
